course: dictionary
lesson: 14912
title: Redeemed: swapping and making up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Redeemed» has more than one meaning, so context clues help you choose the right one. Look at what is being swapped, paid back, or made up for.
explain: At the shop, you redeem a voucher or points to get a prize. «Mia redeemed her voucher for a free ice cream.» The voucher is swapped for something, so redeemed means exchanged.
explain: In a story, a character who is redeemed makes up for a past wrong. «After he broke the window, Sam redeemed himself by fixing it.» Sam did something good, so people can trust him again. Ask: is something being swapped, or is a mistake being made up for?
fact: Redeem means to swap a voucher, coupon or points for a prize or item.
fact: «Redeemed» can also mean made up for a mistake, so that someone seems good again.
fact: «Redeemed himself» means he did something good to make up for a wrong he did.
fact: In church, Christians believe Jesus redeemed people, meaning he saved them from sin.
exercise: «Mia redeemed her shop voucher for a free ice cream.» What did Mia do with the voucher? | Lost it under the sofa | *Swapped it for a free ice cream | Tore it into small pieces | Gave it to her teacher
exercise: «After he broke the window, Sam redeemed himself by fixing it.» What does redeemed himself mean? | Bought a new house | *Made up for his mistake | Broke the window again | Forgot about the window
exercise: «The library lets you redeem reading points for a book.» What can you do with reading points? | *Swap them for a book | Read them out loud | Throw them in the bin | Keep them in a box forever
test: «Grandma redeemed her coupon at the cinema for two tickets.» What did she do with the coupon? | Threw the coupon away | Lent the coupon to a friend | Copied it down on paper | *Swapped the coupon for two tickets
test: «The thief who saved the village was finally redeemed in the last chapter.» What does redeemed suggest? | He was forgotten by everyone | He was made bigger | *He did something good to make up for his past wrongs | He was sent to prison forever
exam: «Ben worked hard for years to help his town after his mistake, and in the end he was redeemed.» What does redeemed mean here? | Lost his job | Paid for a ticket | *Made up for his past wrong, so people saw him as good again | Moved away to a new town
